    </report>
  </then>
</if>

<if>
  <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
    <custom_item>
      type        : OFFLINE_CONFIG_CHECK
      description : "Check offline config for Perfect Forward Secrecy"
      context     : "net ipsec ipsec-policy"
      regex       : "ike-phase2-perfect-forward-secrecy"
      not_expect  : "ike-phase2-perfect-forward-secrecy none"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      description    : "Check REST API for Perfect Forward Secrecy"
      f5_command     : "/tm/net/ipsec/ipsec-policy"
      json_transform : ".items[] | \"\(.name): \(.ikePhase2PerfectForwardSecrecy)\""
      not_expect     : ".*: none"
    </custom_item>
  </condition>

  <then>
    <report type:"PASSED">
      description : "F5BI-VN-300044 - The F5 BIG-IP appliance IPsec VPN Gateway must specify Perfect Forward Secrecy (PFS) during Internet Key Exchange (IKE) negotiation."
      info        : "PFS generates each new encryption key independently from the previous key. Without PFS, compromise of one key will compromise all communications.

The phase 2 (Quick Mode) Security Association (SA) is used to create an IPsec session key. Hence, its rekey or key regeneration procedure is very important. The phase 2 rekey can be performed with or without PFS. With PFS, every time a new IPsec Security Association is negotiated during the Quick Mode, a new Diffie-Hellman (DH) exchange occurs. The new DH shared secret will be included with original keying material (SYKEID_d, initiator nonce, and responder nonce) from phase 1 for generating a new IPsec session key. If PFS is not used, the IPsec session key will always be completely dependent on the original keying material from the Phase-1. Hence, if an older key is compromised at any time, it is possible that all new keys may be compromised.

The DH exchange is performed in the same manner as was done in phase 1 (Main or Aggressive Mode). However, the phase 2 exchange is protected by encrypting the phase 2 packets with the key derived from the phase 1 negotiation. Because DH negotiations during phase 2 are encrypted, the new IPsec session key has an added element of secrecy."
      solution    : "From the BIG-IP GUI:
1. Network
2. IPsec.
3. IPsec Policies.
4. Click on the name of the IPsec Policy.
5. Select any value other than \"NONE\" in \"IKE Phase 2 >> Perfect Forward Secrecy\".
6. Click \"Update\"."
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.8,800-171r3|03.13.08,800-53|SC-8,800-53r5|SC-8,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-002418,CN-L3|8.1.2.2(a),CN-L3|8.1.2.2(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.4.8(a),CN-L3|8.2.4.5(c),CN-L3|8.2.4.5(d),CN-L3|8.5.2.2,CSF|PR.DS-2,CSF|PR.DS-5,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,DISA_Benchmark|F5_BIG-IP_TMOS_VPN_STIG,GDPR|32.1.a,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(e)(1),HIPAA|164.312(e)(2)(i),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.10,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.14,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ITSG-33|SC-8,ITSG-33|SC-8a.,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,NESA|T4.5.1,NESA|T4.5.2,NESA|T7.3.3,NESA|T7.4.1,NIAv2|IE8,NIAv2|IE9,NIAv2|IE12,NIAv2|NS29,NIAv2|SS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.3,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|4.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|2.2.7,PCI-DSSv4.0|4.2.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,Rule-ID|SV-266288r1024921_rule,STIG-ID|F5BI-VN-300044,Vuln-ID|V-266288"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_F5_BIG-IP_TMOS_Y24M09_STIG.zip"
      show_output : YES
    </report>
  </then>
</if>

